 • Glutamic acid is coded by GAA or GAG.  
 • Lysine is coded by AAA or AAG.  
 • For example, changing GAA → AAA requires substituting the first base (G → A).  
  ⇒ Minimum substitutions: 1

2. Leucine (Leu) → Alanine (Ala)  
 • Leucine codons include UUA, UUG, CUU, CUC, CUA, CUG.  
 • Alanine codons are GCU, GCC, GCA, GCG.  
 • Checking pairs (for instance, CUU vs. GCA: differences at positions 1 and 2) shows that the best you can do is change two bases.  
  ⇒ Minimum substitutions: 2

3. Phenylalanine (Phe) → Glycine (Gly)  
 • Phenylalanine is coded by UUU or UUC.  
 • Glycine is coded by GGU, GGC, GGA, GGG.  
 • For example, comparing UUC with GGC yields differences in the first two bases (U → G and U → G) while the third base is the same.  
  ⇒ Minimum substitutions: 2

4. Tryptophan (Trp) → Serine (Ser)  
 • Tryptophan is coded only by UGG.  
 • Serine codons include UCU, UCC, UCA, UCG, AGU, AGC.  
 • For example, UGG can be changed to UCG by substituting the second base (G → C).  
  ⇒ Minimum substitutions: 1

5. Valine (Val) → Glutamic acid (Glu)  
 • Valine codons: GUU, GUC, GUA, GUG.  
 • Glutamic acid codons: GAA, GAG.  
 • For example, GUG can be converted to GAG by substituting the second base (U → A).  
  ⇒ Minimum substitutions: 1

Summarizing the results:  
a) Glutamic acid → Lysine: 1 substitution  
b) Leucine → Alanine: 2 substitutions  
c) Phenylalanine → Glycine: 2 substitutions  
d) Tryptophan → Serine: 1 substitution  
e) Valine → Glutamic acid: 1 substitution

