Low‐speed (or differential) centrifugation works by pelleting large, dense particles while leaving smaller, less dense ones in solution. In a cell homogenate, for example, nuclei (being very large) pellet at low speeds, whereas smaller vesicles (like secretory vesicles) remain in the supernatant. Similarly, free DNA—because of its large size—is heavy enough to sediment at low speeds, while smaller RNA molecules (such as mRNA) stay in solution.

Looking at the options:

• A. Golgi apparatus and endoplasmic reticulum – Both are part of the microsomal fraction and have similar sedimentation properties; they are not separated by low‐speed centrifugation.

• B. Lysosomes and endosomes – Both are membrane‐bound vesicles of comparable size and density and sediment together.

• C. Plasma membrane and cytoskeleton – In a crude homogenate the fragments of the plasma membrane and the insoluble cytoskeletal elements are both found in the pellet; they are not resolved by low‐speed centrifugation.

• D. DNA and mRNA – Free DNA is large and will pellet, whereas mRNA, being much smaller, remains in the supernatant. This pair can be separated by low‐speed centrifugation.

• E. Proteins and lipids – Both are small molecules that stay in solution under low‐speed conditions and are not separated.

• F. Nuclei and secretory vesicles – Nuclei are very large and pellet at low speeds, while the smaller secretory vesicles remain in the supernatant. This pair is also separated by low‐speed centrifugation.

• G. DNA and proteins – Although free DNA (being large) will pellet while many proteins (if they remain soluble) stay in solution, this separation is not as commonly demonstrated in differential centrifugation as the separations in D and F.

Thus, the two pairs that can be separated by performing low‐speed centrifugation are:

  D. DNA and mRNA  
  F. Nuclei and secretory vesicles
