Reaction (3) gives HCl(aq) from H₂(g) and Cl₂(g) in the form:
 (1/2) H₂(g) + (1/2) Cl₂(g) → HCl(aq)
ΔH = –158.31 kJ/mol.

Since reaction (2) involves 3 moles of HCl(aq), multiply (3) by 3:
 3 × [(1/2) H₂(g) + (1/2) Cl₂(g) → HCl(aq)]
gives
 (3/2) H₂(g) + (3/2) Cl₂(g) → 3 HCl(aq)
with ΔH = 3 × (–158.31) = –474.93 kJ/mol.

Step 2. Combine the reactions to eliminate HCl(aq) and H₂(g).

Now add the modified reaction (3) to reaction (2):

Reaction (2): Dy(s) + 3 HCl(aq) → DyCl₃(aq) + (3/2) H₂(g)   ΔH = –699.43 kJ/mol

Adding:
 (3/2) H₂(g) + (3/2) Cl₂(g) → 3 HCl(aq)       ΔH = –474.93 kJ/mol
 Dy(s) + 3 HCl(aq) → DyCl₃(aq) + (3/2) H₂(g)      ΔH = –699.43 kJ/mol
———————————————————————————————————————————————
The 3 HCl(aq) and (3/2) H₂(g) cancel, yielding:
 Dy(s) + (3/2) Cl₂(g) → DyCl₃(aq)
with total ΔH = –474.93 – 699.43 = –1174.36 kJ/mol.

Step 3. Convert the aqueous DyCl₃ to solid DyCl₃.

Reaction (1) converts solid to aqueous:
 DyCl₃(s) → DyCl₃(aq)       ΔH = –180.06 kJ/mol.
To obtain the reverse process (i.e. forming solid from aqueous), reverse reaction (1):
 DyCl₃(aq) → DyCl₃(s)       ΔH = +180.06 kJ/mol.

Now add this to the previous result:
 Dy(s) + (3/2) Cl₂(g) → DyCl₃(aq)     ΔH = –1174.36 kJ/mol
 DyCl₃(aq) → DyCl₃(s)           ΔH = +180.06 kJ/mol
———————————————————————————————————————————————
This gives:
 Dy(s) + (3/2) Cl₂(g) → DyCl₃(s)
with ΔH = –1174.36 + 180.06 = –994.30 kJ/mol.

Thus, the standard enthalpy of formation of solid DyCl₃ is –994.3 kJ/mol, which corresponds to option G.
