We start with the fact that in a β⁺ decay the energy released (the Q‐value) is given by

  Q = [M(parent) – M(daughter) – 2mₑ]c²

Here the “masses” are the nuclidic masses (i.e. the masses of the nuclei, not including the electrons). For the decay

  ¹³N → ¹³C + e⁺ + νₑ

the daughter nucleus is ¹³C and the extra 2mₑ comes from the fact that a positron (with mass mₑ) is emitted and the atomic (or nuclidic) masses of ¹³N and ¹³C are defined for neutral atoms (which include electrons). In β⁺ decay the energy available to the positron (its maximum kinetic energy) is

  Q_max = M(¹³N) – M(¹³C) – 2mₑ

We are told that Q_max = 1.19 MeV. To find the nuclidic mass of ¹³N we rearrange the equation:

  M(¹³N) = Q_max + M(¹³C) + 2mₑ

We now need the nuclidic mass of ¹³C. The atomic mass of ¹³C is about 13.003355 amu. However, this atomic mass includes the mass of the electrons. For ¹³C (which has Z = 6) the electron mass correction is

  6 × mₑ ≈ 6 × 0.00054858 amu ≈ 0.0032915 amu

Thus the nuclidic mass of ¹³C (the mass of the nucleus alone) is

  M(¹³C)_nuc = 13.003355 amu – 0.0032915 amu ≈ 13.0000635 amu

Next, we must convert the Q-value into atomic mass units. Using

  1 amu ≈ 931.494 MeV/c²

we have

  Q_max (in amu) = 1.19 MeV / 931.494 MeV/amu ≈ 0.001278 amu

Also, the mass of two electrons is

  2mₑ ≈ 2 × 0.00054858 amu ≈ 0.00109716 amu

Now we can add these contributions:

  M(¹³N) = 0.001278 amu + 13.0000635 amu + 0.00109716 amu
       ≈ 13.00243866 amu

When we compare this result with the given options, we see that it is closest to option I (13.00267 amu).

Thus, the nuclidic mass of ¹³N is approximately 13.00267 amu.
