This subroutine implements the Euclidean algorithm to compute the greatest common divisor (GCD) of two positive integers I and J. Here’s a step‐by‐step explanation of what the code does:

1. The first IF statement compares I and J. If I is greater than J, the program swaps the two values so that I becomes the smaller number. This is important because the Euclidean algorithm assumes that the divisor is not larger than the dividend.

2. Next, the code enters a loop (starting at label 2) where it calculates the remainder L of dividing I by J using the expression L = I - (I/J)*J. The integer division (I/J) truncates any fraction, so this effectively computes the remainder.

3. An IF statement then checks whether the remainder L is zero. If L is not zero (i.e., L > 0), the algorithm continues by updating I to the old value of J and J to the remainder L (at label 3) and loops back to repeat the process.

4. When the remainder L becomes zero, the current value of J holds the greatest common divisor. The program then assigns J to K and returns.

Thus, the subroutine finds the greatest common divisor of I and J and stores the result in K.

