Monopolistic competition is characterized by many firms offering differentiated products, which gives each firm some degree of market power. Because of this market power, each firm faces a downward-sloping demand curve. As a result, when they maximize profit (by setting marginal revenue equal to marginal cost), the price they charge (P) is greater than the marginal cost (MC).

Let's briefly review the options:

A. P = MC – In perfect competition, firms set P = MC, but in monopolistic competition, due to market power, P > MC.

B. Perfect competition – This is a different market structure with homogeneous products and many firms, so it's not characteristic of monopolistic competition.

C. Efficiency – Monopolistic competition is not efficient because firms do not produce at the lowest point on the average cost curve (and P > MC leads to deadweight loss).

D. Inelastic demand – While the demand curve is downward sloping, it is not necessarily inelastic. Elasticity can vary.

E. P = MR – In monopolistic competition, because the demand curve is downward sloping, marginal revenue (MR) is less than price (P).

F. Perfect knowledge – This is a feature of perfect competition, not monopolistic competition.

G. Mostly price competition – Firms in monopolistic competition also compete on product features, quality, and advertising rather than just price.

H. P > MC – This is a key characteristic of monopolistic competition due to the market power each firm holds.

I. Single seller – This describes a monopoly, not monopolistic competition.

Thus, the correct answer is:

H. P > MC.
