When an excise tax is imposed on producers, the tax burden (or incidence) is divided between consumers and producers based on the relative price elasticities of demand and supply. The general rule is that the side of the market that is less elastic (i.e., less responsive to price changes) will bear a larger share of the tax burden.

Let’s briefly review the key options:

• If both demand and supply are inelastic (or both are elastic), the tax burden is shared between consumers and producers.

• If demand is perfectly elastic, consumers are extremely responsive to price changes. In such cases, producers will have to bear most or all of the tax burden because any attempt to pass the tax on would lead to a loss of all sales.

• Conversely, if demand is perfectly inelastic, consumers are unresponsive to price changes. This means that producers can pass the full tax onto consumers without affecting the quantity demanded.

Now, consider option F:
 “F. If the demand curve is perfectly inelastic and the supply curve is price elastic.”

In this scenario, because the demand is perfectly inelastic, consumers will absorb the full amount of the tax regardless of the elasticity of supply. The fact that the supply curve is elastic means producers can adjust their quantity supplied, but they cannot avoid passing the tax onto consumers when demand is unresponsive. Therefore, consumers end up paying the largest burden of the excise tax.

