To determine which demand curve is the least elastic, recall that elasticity measures how much the quantity demanded changes in response to a change in price. When there are fewer or no substitutes available, consumers have limited options if the price rises, making demand less sensitive (i.e., more inelastic).

Let’s briefly review the options:

• A. Good with many close substitutes – Highly elastic.
• B. Oligopoly with a differentiated product – Somewhat elastic.
• C. Monopolistic competition – Relatively elastic due to many alternatives.
• D. Perfect competition – Perfectly elastic demand.
• E. Luxury good – Typically more elastic.
• F. Necessity good – Usually inelastic, but some necessities might still have substitutes.
• G. Highly substitutable good – Highly elastic.
• H. Good with no close substitutes – Consumers have no alternatives, so demand is very inelastic.
• I. Monopoly firm's output – The monopoly faces the market demand curve, which can be inelastic if the product has no close substitutes.
• J. Oligopoly with a homogeneous product – More elastic than a monopoly’s output when substitutes exist.

Among these, the demand curve that is likely to be the least elastic is for a good with no close substitutes (Option H). This is because consumers have no alternative choices if the price increases, making them less responsive to price changes.

Thus, the answer is:

**H. Demand for a good with no close substitutes**

