 • This is not a defining property of pure public goods. While sometimes private entities may attempt to capture benefits, the inherent characteristic of a pure public good is nonexcludability and nonrivalry.

G. The good cannot be divided into discrete units.
 • This is not a standard characteristic used to define pure public goods. The key properties are nonrivalry and nonexcludability rather than indivisibility.

H. The marginal cost of providing an additional unit of the good to additional citizens is greater than zero.
 • This is false. As noted in option A, the marginal cost is zero due to nonrival consumption.

I. Public goods can only be consumed by those who pay for them.
 • This is false. Because public goods are nonexcludable, people can benefit from them regardless of whether they pay.

J. Public goods are always provided by government agencies.
 • This is false. While governments often provide public goods, they can also be provided by private entities or non-governmental organizations.

Thus, the only true statement about a pure public good is:

A. The marginal cost of providing an additional unit of the good to additional citizens is zero.
