We will solve the problem in two parts.

──────────────────────────────
Part (a): Finding the No‐Load Speed

A DC shunt motor’s speed is governed by the back‐EMF equation

  E = K·φ·n

where
  E = applied voltage minus the drop in the armature (i.e. E = V – IₐRₐ),
  φ = flux per pole,
  n = speed, and
  K is a constant.

At no load the current is very small so that
  E_no = V – Iₙₒ·Rₐ  with φ_no = flux at no load.

At full load the motor carries a larger armature current and the armature reaction reduces the flux. We are told that the flux at full load is 2% less than at no load, i.e.
  φ_FL = 0.98 φ_no.

Also, at full load
  E_FL = V – I_FL·Rₐ,
and the speed is n_FL.

Thus, writing the speed in terms of back‐EMF and flux we have

  n_no = (E_no)/(K φ_no)  and  n_FL = (E_FL)/(K (0.98 φ_no)).

