We start by recalling that the reflection coefficient Γ is given by

  Γ = (ZL – Z0) / (ZL + Z0)

where ZL is the load impedance and Z0 is the characteristic impedance of the line.

For a 75‐ohm load on a 50‐ohm line:
  Γ = (75 – 50) / (75 + 50) = 25/125 = 0.2

The fraction of the incident power that is reflected is |Γ|²:
  |Γ|² = (0.2)² = 0.04, or 4%

Thus, if 50W of power is incident on the 75‐ohm load, 4% (which is 2W) is reflected and the remaining 96% (48W) is delivered to the load.

Now, if instead a 50‐ohm load is used (i.e. a matched load), then
  Γ = (50 – 50) / (50 + 50) = 0/100 = 0
so no power is reflected. In this case the entire 50W is delivered to the load.

Among the given options, the one that correctly states that a 75‐ohm load reflects 4% of the power and that a matched (50‐ohm) load would have all 50W delivered is:

  H. 4 percent of the power is reflected; source supplies all the 50W power to the load with 50 ohm

(Note: The “4 percent” refers to the mismatched case; for a matched load, 0% is reflected.)
