We start with the ideal gas law written in the form

  P · V = m · R_specific · T

where
  P = pressure,
  V = volume,
  m = mass of the gas,
  R_specific = specific gas constant for air, and
  T = absolute temperature.

Step 1. Convert the given units to consistent English units.

• Pressure: The given pressure is 17 lb/in². To convert to lb_f/ft², note that
  1 lb/in² = 144 lb_f/ft².
Thus,
  P = 17 lb/in² × 144 lb_f/ft² per lb/in² = 2448 lb_f/ft².

• Volume: The chamber measures 35 ft × 20 ft × 10 ft, so
  V = 35 ft × 20 ft × 10 ft = 7000 ft³.

• Temperature: The given temperature is 75°F. In Rankine (°R) use
  T(°R) = T(°F) + 460.
Thus,
  T = 75 + 460 = 535 °R.

• Specific Gas Constant: For air, a commonly used value is
  R_specific = 53.35 ft·lbf/(lbm·°R).

Step 2. Solve for mass m.

Rearrange the ideal gas law to solve for m:
  m = (P · V) / (R_specific · T).

Plug in the numbers:
  m = (2448 lb_f/ft² × 7000 ft³) / (53.35 ft·lbf/(lbm·°R) × 535 °R).

Calculate the numerator:
  2448 × 7000 = 17,136,000 (lb_f·ft).

Calculate the denominator:
  53.35 × 535 ≈ 28,542.25 (ft·lbf/lbm).

Thus,
  m ≈ 17,136,000 / 28,542.25 ≈ 600 lbm.

Step 3. Choose the correct option.

The calculated mass is approximately 600 lbm, which is closest to option C: 601 lbm.

Final Answer: 601 lbm.
