We start by recalling that the van der Waals equation is

  (P + a(n/V)²)(V – nb) = nRT

where
  • a (with units L²·atm/mol²) accounts for intermolecular attractions,
  • b (with units L/mol) accounts for the finite volume of the gas molecules.

The critical temperature (Tc) for a van der Waals gas is given by

  Tc = (8a)/(27Rb)

which shows that Tc is directly proportional to a and inversely proportional to b. In other words, the gas with the highest ratio a/b will have the highest critical temperature.

The table gives:
  Gas A: a = 4.0, b = 0.027
  Gas B: a = 12.0, b = 0.030
  Gas C: a = 6.0, b = 0.032

Step 1. Determine the gas with the highest critical temperature.
  Calculate a/b for each gas:
  • Gas A: 4.0/0.027 ≈ 148.15
  • Gas B: 12.0/0.030 = 400
  • Gas C: 6.0/0.032 = 187.5
  Thus, Gas B (a/b = 400) has the highest critical temperature.

Step 2. Identify the gas with the largest molecular volume.
  The constant b represents the effective volume excluded by a mole of molecules. Comparing the b values:
  • Gas A: 0.027 L/mol
  • Gas B: 0.030 L/mol
  • Gas C: 0.032 L/mol
  Gas C has the largest b, meaning it has the largest molecular volume.

Step 3. Find the gas that behaves most ideally around STP.
  A gas behaves more ideally when the corrections (a and b) are small. Comparing the values:
  • Gas A: a = 4.0 (smallest) and b = 0.027 (smallest)
  • Gas B: a = 12.0 and b = 0.030
  • Gas C: a = 6.0 and b = 0.032
  Gas A, having the smallest values for both a and b, will show the most ideal behavior around STP.

Thus, the answers are:
  (a) Gas B has the highest critical temperature.
  (b) Gas C has the largest molecular volume.
  (c) Gas A behaves most ideally around STP.

Comparing with the given options, this corresponds to:

Option B: Gas B has the highest critical temperature, Gas C has the largest molecular volume, Gas A has the most ideal general behavior around STP.
