Addison’s disease (primary adrenal insufficiency) is characterized by a deficiency in the hormones produced by the adrenal cortex, notably cortisol and aldosterone. One of the hallmark features of Addison’s disease is hyperpigmentation (generalized pigmentation) of the skin. This occurs because the pituitary gland produces more ACTH when cortisol levels are low, and ACTH can stimulate melanin production.

Let’s briefly review the options:

A. Results in frequent urination and thirst  
 • Addison’s disease typically does not cause polyuria (frequent urination) or polydipsia (increased thirst).  
B. It is a result of an overactive thyroid gland  
 • Addison’s disease is related to adrenal dysfunction, not an overactive thyroid.  
C. High cholesterol levels are a common symptom  
 • There is no established link between Addison’s disease and high cholesterol levels.  
D. It is a recognized cause of hypertension  
 • Addison’s disease usually causes low blood pressure (hypotension) rather than hypertension.  
E. It is caused by excessive production of insulin  
 • Addison’s disease is due to adrenal insufficiency, not insulin overproduction.  
F. It is a disease that affects the pituitary gland  
 • The primary defect in Addison’s disease lies in the adrenal glands, not the pituitary.  
G. It is a form of cancer that affects the adrenal glands  
 • Addison’s disease is not cancer; it is usually due to autoimmune destruction of the adrenal cortex.  
H. Diabetes is a complication  
 • While Addison’s disease can be part of autoimmune polyendocrine syndromes that may include type 1 diabetes, diabetes is not a direct complication of Addison’s disease.  
I. Leads to generalized pigmentation  
 • This is true; increased ACTH production leads to hyperpigmentation of the skin.  
J. It is due to a deficiency of prolactin hormone  
 • Addison’s disease results from adrenal insufficiency, not a deficiency of prolactin.

Therefore, the correct statement about Addison’s disease is:

I. Leads to generalized pigmentation

