Let’s analyze the scenario:

• The franchise imports and distributes bicycles.  
• Two types of property arrive separately at a county warehouse:  
 – Bike tires, which are stored (mingled and stacked) in the warehouse along with other tires.  
 – Assembled bicycles, which remain on the loading docks in their shipping crates.

Ad valorem property taxes are generally imposed on tangible property that is located in the taxing jurisdiction. The key is whether the property is “sited” or “located” in the county at the time of assessment.

• The tires are stored in the warehouse—a fixed, designated location in the county. Because they are mingled with other tires, they are clearly held in the county and can be considered part of the taxable inventory.  
• The bicycles, however, are kept in their shipping crates on the loading docks. Although the loading docks are part of the distribution facility, the fact that the bicycles remain in their crates suggests they are not yet integrated into the warehouse’s inventory. In many legal contexts, property that is still in transit (or not stored in a fixed location within the county) may not be subject to local ad valorem taxation.

Thus, only the tires, which are stored in the warehouse, meet the criteria for proper assessment under the county’s ad valorem tax.

The correct answer is:

H. tires only.

