Let’s break down the situation and each statement:

The contract provided by the mother requires that the photographer “produce a minimum of six acceptable photographs by August 15” in exchange for a $200 fee. In contracts like this, it is generally understood that:

• The photographer’s performance is conditioned on the mother’s cooperation in arranging for the session.
• There is an implied duty on the part of the mother to cooperate reasonably and in good faith.
• If the mother fails to cooperate, the photographer may be excused from further performance and may have a right to claim damages.
• The photographer is not required to produce more than what is explicitly stated in the contract unless the contract provides for additional deliverables.

Now, let’s review each option briefly:

A. “By entering into the contract as written, the photographer assumed the risk that the mother would fail to cooperate…”  
 – This is inaccurate because it is the mother who must cooperate; the photographer is not assuming the risk of non-cooperation.

B. “The mother was under an implied duty to act in good faith to reasonably cooperate…”  
 – This is correct.

C. “The mother had a legal obligation to ensure the two-year-old was cooperative during the photography session.”  
 – While it might be an expectation that the mother facilitates the session, the wording “ensure” is strong. However, in practice, the mother is responsible for making arrangements, so this is not as clearly off base as some other options.

D. “The mother's failure to cooperate would result in a full refund of the $200 payment.”  
 – This is problematic because if the mother fails to cooperate (thereby breaching the implied duty), it generally excuses the photographer’s performance. Typically, the non-breaching party (here, the photographer) is not forced to refund the fee; in fact, the photographer might be entitled to keep the fee or claim damages. The “full refund” is not an automatic outcome.

E. “An implied condition of the photographer's duty of performance was that the mother reasonably cooperate…”  
 – This is correct.

F. “The mother's refusal to cooperate reasonably in arranging for the taking of the photographs would excuse the photographer from further obligation under the contract and also give the photographer a right of action against the mother for breach of contract.”  
 – This is correct.

G. “The photographer had an implied duty to ensure the photographs were of a professional quality.”  
 – This is correct given the nature of the service.

H. “The photographer had a right to cancel the contract if the mother was not cooperating…”  
 – This is generally correct under the doctrine of “anticipatory breach” or failure of a condition precedent.

I. “The photographer was under an obligation to take more than six photographs if the mother was not satisfied with the initial results.”  
 – This statement is clearly at odds with the contract. The contract specifically requires only a minimum of six acceptable photographs. There is no implied obligation to produce additional photographs if the mother is unsatisfied.

While Options A, D, and I all contain inaccuracies, Option I is the least accurate because it imposes an obligation on the photographer that is not supported by the contract at all. The contract does not state that the photographer must produce extra photographs beyond the specified six if the mother is dissatisfied.

Therefore, the LEAST accurate statement is:

I. The photographer was under an obligation to take more than six photographs if the mother was not satisfied with the initial results.

