**Answer: E. The farmer's promise to survey the tract was an unfulfilled condition precedent to the tenant's right to purchase.**

**Explanation:**

- The lease contained an option for the tenant to purchase 10 acres for $150,000 at the expiration of the lease term.
- Before the lease was executed, the farmer orally promised to have the 10-acre tract surveyed. Although this promise was not incorporated into the written lease, it was intended to define or clarify the tract to be purchased.
- In contract law, an oral promise made prior to the execution of a written contract can sometimes be interpreted as a collateral promise or, more critically, as a condition precedent. A condition precedent is an event or action that must occur before a party is obligated to perform.
- In this case, if the survey was intended as a condition precedent to the tenant’s right to exercise the option, then the tenant’s right to purchase depends on the farmer first having the tract surveyed.
- Since the farmer did not have the tract surveyed, the condition precedent was not fulfilled. Consequently, the tenant’s claim for specific performance (i.e., forcing the sale under the option) is weakened because the necessary condition was not met.
- This defense is the most compelling because it directly addresses the contractual basis on which the tenant’s right to purchase was predicated.

