Below is an analysis based on general principles of real property law and covenant enforcement:

Background of the Situation
• A 1,000‐acre farmland was subdivided into four 250‑acre parcels.
• The original deed between the farmer and the development company contained a restriction (expressly binding on “all subsequent grantees, their heirs, and assigns”) that any further subdivisions must be limited to minimum two‑acre lots for single‑family dwelling units only.
• The development company immediately subdivided two of the parcels into lots (3, 4, and 5 acres) and sold them with the restriction (phase 1).
• Later, the remaining 250‑acre parcel was subdivided by a contractor into one‑acre lots (phase 2) without including the restriction in the deeds.
• Subsequently, an investor purchased a different 250‑acre parcel from the farmer’s estate and built a large shopping center in an area that was rezoned for commercial and industrial uses.
• Now, a phase 1 homeowner is selling his lot to two physicians who plan to start a suburban medical practice—a use that is clearly not a single‑family dwelling unit.

Key Issues
1. Covenant Scope and Intent:  
 – The original covenant was intended to preserve a residential character by restricting subdivision to lots of at least two acres for single‑family dwellings.  
 – However, the contractor’s subdivision (phase 2) and the investor’s commercial shopping center demonstrate that not all of the parcels have been kept to that residential standard.

2. Changed Circumstances:  
 – The rezoning of the surrounding area for commercial and industrial uses, along with the development of a major shopping center, evidences a significant change in the character of the neighborhood.  
 – Under the doctrine of changed circumstances, a covenant that was once viable may become unenforceable if the surrounding area has transformed in such a way that the covenant no longer serves its original purpose.

3. Enforceability Against a Commercial Enterprise:  
 – Although a suburban medical practice is clearly a commercial enterprise (and not a single‑family dwelling), the fact that the neighborhood has evolved (with phase 2 and the shopping center) undermines the rationale for enforcing the covenant as originally intended.

Conclusion
Given that the surrounding area has changed dramatically—with a shopping center, extensive commercial zoning, and a subdivision that did not include the covenant—the court is likely to find that the original residential restriction no longer has practical effect. In other words, the covenant is rendered obsolete by the changed character of the neighborhood.

Thus, in an action by the homeowners in phase 1 to prevent the physicians’ commercial use, the court will most likely hold that:

I. the restrictions would no longer be enforceable, because of the change in the character of the neighborhood.

