The exponential distribution with mean 3 has rate parameter λ = 1/3. For an exponential distribution, the variance is the square of the mean, so Var(X) = 3² = 9.

Given a sample of size n = 36, the sample mean Ẋ has:
 • Mean: μ₍Ẋ₎ = 3
 • Variance: σ²₍Ẋ₎ = 9/36 = 0.25, hence standard deviation σ₍Ẋ₎ = 0.5

Using the Central Limit Theorem, we approximate Ẋ by a normal distribution N(3, 0.25).

We want to find P(2.5 ≤ Ẋ ≤ 4). Standardize the endpoints:

For X = 2.5:
 Z = (2.5 - 3) / 0.5 = -0.5 / 0.5 = -1

For X = 4:
 Z = (4 - 3) / 0.5 = 1 / 0.5 = 2

Thus,
 P(2.5 ≤ Ẋ ≤ 4) = P(-1 ≤ Z ≤ 2) = Φ(2) - Φ(-1)

Using standard normal distribution values:
 Φ(2) ≈ 0.9772
 Φ(-1) ≈ 0.1587

Therefore,
 P(2.5 ≤ Ẋ ≤ 4) ≈ 0.9772 - 0.1587 = 0.8185

This corresponds to option I.
