We analyze each statement one by one.

Statement 1: “For n > 1, the set {1,2, …, n–1} is a group under multiplication modulo n.”

• A group under multiplication modulo n is the set of all numbers relatively prime to n (the multiplicative group of units modulo n). When n is prime, every number 1, 2, …, n–1 is relatively prime to n, so the set is indeed a group. However, for composite n the set {1,2,…,n–1} contains nonunits (numbers that share a common factor with n) and thus is not a group. Since the statement claims this holds for every n > 1, it is false.

Statement 2: “There is an integer x such that 63x mod 100 = 1.”

• This is asking if 63 has a multiplicative inverse modulo 100. For an inverse to exist, we must have gcd(63, 100) = 1. Since 63 factors as 3²·7 and 100 factors as 2²·5², they share no common factors. Therefore, gcd(63, 100) = 1 and an inverse exists. In fact, using the extended Euclidean algorithm one finds that x = 27 is a solution because 63·27 = 1701 and 1701 mod 100 = 1.

Now, reviewing the options:
• Options A, C, and E all correctly state that Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true.
• Option E adds “and x is a prime number” which is incorrect because 27 is not prime.
• Option C states “and x is an odd number.” Since x = 27 is indeed odd, this is the correct additional property.

Thus, the best answer is:

C. False, True and x is an odd number.
