Let the two integers be 45 and b. Denote d = gcd(45, b). We know that

  lcm(45, b) = (45 × b) / d.

According to the problem, dividing the lcm by the gcd gives 33:
  (lcm) / d = 33  ⟹  (45 × b) / (d²) = 33.

Rearrange to solve for b:
  45 × b = 33 × d²  ⟹  b = (33 × d²) / 45 = (11 × d²) / 15.

Since b must be an integer and d is a divisor of 45, we list the divisors of 45: 1, 3, 5, 9, 15, and 45.

Now, test each value for d to see when b is an integer:

• If d = 1: b = (11 × 1²) / 15 = 11/15 (not an integer).

• If d = 3: b = (11 × 9) / 15 = 99/15 = 6.6 (not an integer).

• If d = 5: b = (11 × 25) / 15 = 275/15 ≈ 18.33 (not an integer).

• If d = 9: b = (11 × 81) / 15 = 891/15 = 59.4 (not an integer).

• If d = 15: b = (11 × 225) / 15 = 2475/15 = 165 (an integer).

• If d = 45: b = (11 × 2025) / 15 = 22275/15 = 1485 (an integer, but larger than 165).

We are asked for the smallest possible value of b, which is 165.

Thus, the answer is 165.
