In any triangle, a midsegment is parallel to the third side and its length is half that of the third side. In triangle RST:

• X is the midpoint of RS, Y is the midpoint of RT, and Z is the midpoint of ST.
• The segment XY is a midsegment connecting the midpoints of RS and RT. By the midsegment theorem, XY is parallel to ST and has length (½)·ST. Since XY = 7, it follows that ST = 14.
• The segment XZ is a midsegment connecting the midpoints of RS and ST. Therefore, XZ is parallel to RT and its length is (½)·RT. Given RT = 13, we have XZ = 13/2 = 6.5.

The measure of angle YXZ (124°) confirms the parallel relationships, but the key fact is that XZ is half of RT.

Thus, the length of side XZ is 6.5, which corresponds to option B.
