We wish to evaluate

  L = limₙ→∞ [2/√π · √n · ∫₀¹ (1 − x²)ⁿ dx].

For large n the integrand (1 − x²)ⁿ decays very rapidly except when x is very close to 0. This suggests that the main contribution to the integral comes from the neighborhood of x = 0. We can use this idea to approximate the integral.

Step 1. Change of Variable

Let x be small and write
  (1 − x²)ⁿ ≈ exp[n ln(1 − x²)].
For small x, ln(1 − x²) ≈ −x², so
  (1 − x²)ⁿ ≈ exp(−n x²).

Thus, for large n, the integral behaves like
  ∫₀¹ (1 − x²)ⁿ dx ≈ ∫₀∞ exp(−n x²) dx.

Step 2. Evaluate the Approximate Integral

The Gaussian integral is well known:
  ∫₀∞ exp(−n x²) dx = ½ · √(π/n).

Step 3. Combine with the Prefactor

Now, substitute the approximation back into the limit:
  L ≈ limₙ→∞ [2/√π · √n · (½ · √(π/n))]
     = limₙ→∞ [2/√π · √n · √π/(2√n)]
     = limₙ→∞ [ (√n/√n) · (2/2) ]
     = 1.

Thus, the limit is 1.

Among the given options, this corresponds to option I. 1.0.
