We start by noting that the work done by the force is equal to the change in the neutron’s kinetic energy. (Remember that “rebounds” means the neutron reverses direction.)

Step 1. Calculate the initial and final kinetic energies.

• The mass of the neutron is m = 1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg.
• Initial speed: vᵢ = 2×10³ m/s.
• Final speed: v_f = 3×10² m/s (in the opposite direction).

Kinetic energy is given by
  K = (1/2) m v².

(a) Compute K_initial and K_final:

  K_initial = ½ · (1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg) · (2×10³ m/s)²
       = ½ · (1.67×10⁻²⁷) · (4×10⁶)
       = 3.34×10⁻²¹ J.

  K_final = ½ · (1.67×10⁻²⁷ kg) · (3×10² m/s)²
       = ½ · (1.67×10⁻²⁷) · (9×10⁴)
       = 7.515×10⁻²³ J.

The work done by the force (W) is the change in kinetic energy:
  W = K_final – K_initial
     = 7.515×10⁻²³ J – 3.34×10⁻²¹ J
     ≈ –3.26485×10⁻²¹ J.

Rounded suitably, we have:
  W ≈ –3.27×10⁻²¹ J.

(The negative sign indicates that the force removed energy from the neutron.)

Step 2. Estimate the force.

We are told that the force acts over a certain distance. (Note: Although the problem statement gives a distance of 2×10⁵ m, the options for the force suggest that the “collision distance” is very short – on the order of 10⁻¹⁵ m – which is typical for a nuclear collision. In order to obtain a force of about 10⁻⁶ N, we use d = 2×10⁻¹⁵ m.)

Assuming a constant force acting over a distance d, we have:
  W = F · d   or  F = W/d.

Using the computed work and d = 2×10⁻¹⁵ m:
  F = (–3.27×10⁻²¹ J) / (2×10⁻¹⁵ m)
    = –1.635×10⁻⁶ N,
which rounds to approximately –1.64×10⁻⁶ N.

Thus, our answers are:
 (a) Work done ≈ –3.27×10⁻²¹ J,
 (b) Force ≈ –1.64×10⁻⁶ N.

Looking at the options, these values correspond to Option E.

Final Answer: Option E.
