We are given two thin lenses made of crown and flint glass. Their “Abbe numbers” (which are inversely proportional to the dispersive power) are

  v₁ = 60.2 (for the crown glass lens)  
  v₂ = 36.2 (for the flint glass lens).

When two thin lenses are in contact, the combined focal length f is given by

  1/f = 1/f₁ + 1/f₂.

For an achromatic doublet (i.e. one with no net chromatic aberration) the dispersions of the two lenses must cancel. A standard result is that the condition for achromatism is

  (f₁/v₁) + (f₂/v₂) = 0.

This comes from the fact that the focal length of a lens is proportional to 1/(n – 1) and the dispersive behavior is inversely proportional to the Abbe number v.

Step 1. Write the achromatic condition:
  f₁/v₁ + f₂/v₂ = 0  ⟹  f₁ = – (v₁/v₂) f₂.

Let k = v₁/v₂. Then
  f₁ = – k f₂  with  k = 60.2/36.2 ≈ 1.662.

Step 2. Use the lens combination formula. We are told that the combination has focal length f = 10 cm:
  1/10 = 1/f₁ + 1/f₂.

Substitute f₁ = –k f₂:
  1/10 = 1/(–k f₂) + 1/f₂ = –1/(k f₂) + 1/f₂ = (1 – 1/k)/f₂.

Solve for f₂:
  f₂ = 10(1 – 1/k).

Now, calculate 1/k:
  1/k = 1/1.662 ≈ 0.601.
Thus,
  1 – 1/k ≈ 1 – 0.601 = 0.399,
and
  f₂ ≈ 10 × 0.399 = 3.99 cm.

Step 3. Find f₁ using f₁ = –k f₂:
  f₁ ≈ –1.662 × 3.99 ≈ –6.63 cm.

Thus, the focal lengths of the individual lenses are:
  f₂ ≈ 3.99 cm (positive, so a converging lens) and
  f₁ ≈ –6.63 cm (negative, so a diverging lens).

Looking at the options, this result corresponds to Option I:
  f₂ = 3.99 cm, f₁ = –6.63 cm.
