We can think of the polished steel ball bearing as a convex spherical mirror. (Even though steel is opaque, a well‐polished ball bearing can act as a mirror.) For a spherical mirror the focal length is

  f = R/2

where R is the radius of curvature. Since the ball bearing is 1 inch in diameter, its radius is

  R = 0.5 in  →  f = 0.5/2 = 0.25 in.

Because the mirror is convex the focal length is taken as negative (by convention):

  f = –0.25 in.

Now the mirror (or mirror equation) is

  1/f = 1/do + 1/di

Here the object (the 2‐inch–diameter light bulb) is 20 inches away (do = 20 in). Substituting in we have

  1/(–0.25) = 1/20 + 1/di  
  –4 = 0.05 + 1/di  
  1/di = –4 – 0.05 = –4.05

Thus

  di = –1/4.05 ≈ –0.2469 in.

The negative sign tells us that the image is virtual and appears behind the mirror—that is, “beneath the surface” of the ball bearing. (When looking into a convex mirror the image appears to be located behind the mirror’s reflecting surface.)

The magnification m is given by

  m = –di/do ≈ –(–0.2469)/20 = 0.012345

Since the object’s diameter is 2 inches, the image’s diameter is

  (image diameter) = m × (object diameter) ≈ 0.012345 × 2 ≈ 0.02469 in

which is about 0.025 inches.

Among the answer choices, Option J states:

  “0.025 inches in diameter, located at a distance of 0.25 in. beneath the surface of the ball bearing.”

This exactly matches our calculation.

Thus, the correct answer is Option J.
