Let’s analyze the two cases:

─────────────────────────────  
Case 1: Velocity Selector

• In a velocity selector, only particles with a specific velocity v (given by v = E/B) pass through.  
• When these particles enter a uniform magnetic field, the radius of their circular path is given by:  
  r = (m · v) / (q · B)  
• Since v, q, and B are the same for all ions, the radius is directly proportional to the mass:  
  r ∝ m  
• Therefore, for lithium ions of masses m₆ = 6 amu and m₇ = 7 amu, the ratio of radii is:  
  r₆/r₇ = 6/7 ≈ 0.857

─────────────────────────────  
Case 2: Accelerating Electric Field

• In this setup, the ions are accelerated by a strong electric field through a potential difference V.  
• Their kinetic energy becomes:  
  ½ m v² = qV  
  => v = √(2qV/m)  
• When these ions then enter the magnetic field, the radius is:  
  r = (m · v) / (q · B) = m / (qB) · √(2qV/m) = √(m) / (qB) · √(2qV)  
• Here, r is proportional to √m.  
• Thus, the ratio of the radii for the two masses is:  
  r₆/r₇ = √(6/7) ≈ 0.926

─────────────────────────────  
Conclusion

• In the velocity selector case, the radius ratio is 6/7 ≈ 0.857.  
• In the accelerating electric field case, the radius ratio is √(6/7) ≈ 0.926.

Among the given options, the one that matches these values is:

  Option G: 0.857 and 0.926

Thus, the correct answer is Option G.
