We start with the formula for the period of a simple pendulum:

  T = 2π√(L/g)

At 20°C the pendulum is adjusted so that it “beats seconds” (i.e. its period is 2 seconds for a full swing). When the temperature rises to 30°C the iron rod expands. For a rod of length L at 20°C, the new length at 30°C is

  L' = L(1 + αΔT)

where
  α = 16×10⁻⁶ °C⁻¹  and  ΔT = 30°C − 20°C = 10°C.

Thus,
  L' = L(1 + 16×10⁻⁶ × 10) = L(1 + 1.6×10⁻⁴).

Since the period depends on the square root of the length, we have

  T' = 2π√(L'/g) = 2π√((1 + 1.6×10⁻⁴)L/g)
      = 2π√(L/g) √(1 + 1.6×10⁻⁴)
      ≈ T (1 + (1/2)(1.6×10⁻⁴))      [using √(1+δ) ≈ 1 + δ/2 for small δ]
      = T (1 + 0.8×10⁻⁴).

Since T = 2 seconds at 20°C, the new period is

  T' ≈ 2(1 + 0.8×10⁻⁴) seconds.

This means each tick now takes an extra

  ΔT_tick = 2 × 0.8×10⁻⁴ = 1.6×10⁻⁴ seconds

more than it should.

Over one week (7 days), the total real time is

  t_week = 7 × 24 × 3600 = 604800 seconds.

If the clock were perfect, it would register

  N = 604800 / 2 = 302400 ticks.

But because each tick takes a bit longer, the number of ticks the clock actually counts is

  N' = 604800 / [2(1 + 0.8×10⁻⁴)].

Using a first-order approximation for small x (1/(1+x) ≈ 1 − x), we get

  N' ≈ 302400 (1 − 0.8×10⁻⁴)
      = 302400 − 302400 × 0.8×10⁻⁴
      = 302400 − 24.192.

Thus, the clock loses about 24.2 seconds in a week.

Among the given options, the correct answer is:

  E. 24.2 s.
