We can determine the boiling point at a lower pressure using the Clausius–Clapeyron equation. The equation is:

  ln(P₂/P₁) = –(ΔH_vap/R) (1/T₂ – 1/T₁)

where:
 • P₁ = 760 Torr (normal pressure)
 • T₁ = 373.12 K (normal boiling point)
 • P₂ = 253 Torr (pressure on top of Mt. Everest)
 • ΔH_vap = 40.656 kJ/mol = 40656 J/mol
 • R = 8.314 J/(mol·K)

Step 1. Write the equation:
  ln(253/760) = –(40656/8.314) (1/T₂ – 1/373.12)

Step 2. Calculate ln(253/760):
  253/760 ≈ 0.3329
  ln(0.3329) ≈ –1.10

Step 3. Rearrange the equation to solve for 1/T₂:
  –1.10 = –(40656/8.314) (1/T₂ – 1/373.12)
  Multiply both sides by –1:
  1.10 = (40656/8.314) (1/T₂ – 1/373.12)

Step 4. Solve for (1/T₂ – 1/373.12):
  1/T₂ – 1/373.12 = 1.10 / (40656/8.314)
  First, compute 40656/8.314 ≈ 4893.8
  Thus, 1/T₂ – 1/373.12 = 1.10 / 4893.8 ≈ 0.0002249

Step 5. Now, solve for 1/T₂:
  1/T₂ = 1/373.12 + 0.0002249
  1/373.12 ≈ 0.002678
  So, 1/T₂ ≈ 0.002678 + 0.0002249 = 0.0029029

Step 6. Finally, calculate T₂:
  T₂ = 1 / 0.0029029 ≈ 344.4 K

Thus, the boiling point of water on top of Mt. Everest is approximately 344 K.

Comparing with the options given, the answer is:

I. 344 K
