We start by noting that for an opaque surface at thermal equilibrium, the emissivity (ε) and reflectance (ρ) are related by

  ε + ρ = 1.

Thus, when the reflectance of each wall is 0.9, the emissivity is

  ε_old = 1 – 0.9 = 0.1.

Similarly, when the reflectance is reduced to 0.3, the emissivity becomes

  ε_new = 1 – 0.3 = 0.7.

For radiative heat transfer between two parallel surfaces (such as the walls of a thermos), the net radiative exchange (ignoring conduction or convection) is proportional to the effective “exchange factor” F given by

  F = (ε₁ ε₂) / (ε₁ + ε₂ – ε₁ ε₂).

In the case where the two walls have the same emissivity (ε₁ = ε₂ = ε), this simplifies to

  F = (ε²) / (2ε – ε²) = ε / (2 – ε).

Thus, the heat transfer Q is proportional to F, so

  Q ∝ ε / (2 – ε).

Now, let’s compute the proportional factors for the old and new emissivities.

For the old walls:
  F_old ∝ ε_old/(2 – ε_old) = 0.1/(2 – 0.1) = 0.1/1.9 ≈ 0.0526.

For the new walls:
  F_new ∝ ε_new/(2 – ε_new) = 0.7/(2 – 0.7) = 0.7/1.3 ≈ 0.5385.

The increase in the heat transfer factor is then

  Factor Increase = F_new / F_old ≈ 0.5385 / 0.0526 ≈ 10.24.

This value is approximately 10.2, which corresponds to option G.

Thus, reducing the reflectance from 0.9 to 0.3 would increase the radiative heat transfer between the walls by a factor of about 10.2.
