Sherman and Key’s (1932) study of the "Hollow Children" examined the effects of early environmental conditions on intellectual development. In this study, children raised in institutional, deprived settings (often referred to as "hollow children" due to the lack of emotional and cognitive stimulation) were compared with children raised in more enriched, nurturing home environments. The researchers found that children from the deprived, institutionalized settings exhibited significantly lower intellectual performance—evidenced by lower IQ scores and poorer cognitive development—than their peers from stimulating home environments. This finding indicates that environmental factors, particularly the level of early stimulation and nurturance, play a critical role in shaping intellectual development.

