Let’s analyze each statement:

• **A. Excess dopamine or sensitivity to dopamine could be a contributory factor.**  
 This statement is correct. One of the well-established theories in the etiology of schizophrenia is that dysregulation of dopamine—either an excess of dopamine or increased sensitivity to it—plays a role in the development of the disorder.

• **B. A lower rate of Schizophrenia diagnosed among Africa Americans.**  
 This statement is incorrect. Epidemiological studies have generally shown that African Americans are at a higher risk of being diagnosed with schizophrenia compared to whites, not a lower rate.

• **C. Schizophrenia is primarily caused by poor lifestyle choices.**  
 This statement is incorrect. Schizophrenia is a complex condition with a strong genetic component, and while environmental factors (including aspects of lifestyle) may influence its onset, it is not “primarily” caused by lifestyle choices.

• **D. Schizophrenia can be completely cured through psychotherapy.**  
 This statement is incorrect. Schizophrenia is considered a chronic mental health condition. While psychotherapy can be an important part of treatment to help manage symptoms and improve functioning, it is not a cure.

• **E. All schizophrenia patients exhibit severe personality splitting.**  
 This statement is incorrect. Severe personality splitting is not a feature of schizophrenia; it is more commonly associated with dissociative identity disorder. Schizophrenia typically involves symptoms such as hallucinations, delusions, and disorganized thinking, rather than personality splitting.

**Conclusion:**  
The incorrect statements are B, C, D, and E.

